Mark Healey <die@spammer.die> writes:

> I got it running by manually applying certain lines from the Fedora 
> supplied patches.  
> The only problem is that it can't read my .nethackrc.

The game needs to access the file in your home directory, but the binary
is normally set up with suid games, sometimse sgid bin.
So you need to give the world x right to your home directory and r right
to the file.
chmod a+x ~
chmod a+r ~/.nethackrc

Notice that just giving x rights to a directory doesn't give the right to
read the directory listing, just to open files by name to those who
already know the name of the file to read, so this is normally not a big
security risk if you don't keep anything secret directly in your home
directory. If you are paranoid about that, you may want to move your most
sensitive files to some subdirectory that doesn't have x rights given to
the world. 
The .nethackrc file probably doesn't include anything you need to keep
secret.

> I created a wizard user, ran it with the -D option, committed suicide and 
> left a bones file.  I also saved a game.
> The ownership of each is games:[user]
> Isn't that backwards?

Really? My bones files are owned by games, only the group is my default
group. If you have set up the game to be sgid instead of suid, it may be
different. Anyway, the files have permissions set to -rw-rw---- so it is
enough for the game to access them with either user or group rights, it
doesn't need both. And you usually don't have write permissions to the
directory yourself with a normal user, so you can't write or delete them
anyway.
So, that is normal.

